     * that is not a snapshot, or A and B of different platforms or channel
     * counts (nothing sent); [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; the capture's and
     * the recall's errors; [`DspiError::Io`] for an unreadable store or no
     * folder named; [`DspiError::Unsupported`] on the web (use
     * [`DspiCore::ab_start_documents`]).
     */
    fun `abStart`(`aId`: kotlin.String, `bId`: kotlin.String): AbCompare
    
    /**
     * [`DspiCore::ab_start`] with snapshot documents' text (the web, and a
     * client's own store): `a_text` "" captures the device as A.
     */
    fun `abStartDocuments`(`aText`: kotlin.String, `bText`: kotlin.String): AbCompare
    
    /**
     * The running compare, or `active: false` with none (a disconnect
     * ends one). Never fails; no I/O.
     */
    fun `abState`(): AbCompare
    
    /**
     * Recall the other side: one bulk write (`0xA1`) plus the settings
     * outside the image that differ, recorded in the event log as a recall.
     * The side left is captured first (reads only), so edits made on it are
     * kept and come back on the next toggle, as in a DAW's A/B.
     * It ends the undo history; it is not an undo step. Never guarded and
     * never token-gated. Returns the compare, on the new side, with the
     * recall's changes.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] with no compare running; the
     * recall's errors. A lost connection ends the compare
     * ([`DspiError::Disconnected`]); another refusal leaves it on its side.
     */
    fun `abToggle`(): AbCompare
    
    /**
     * The unit's audio link as the connect probe found it, without
     * traffic: `None` when the device has no alink (stock firmware, the fork
     * without it, role 0, a protocol version this build does not speak, or
     * a hub that refused the read). `Disconnected` without a device.
     */
    fun `alinkInfo`(): AlinkInfo?
    
    /**
     * Write the audio link delays and hop offsets the system delay asks for to
     * every unit (`system.align` with `apply`). Each unit re-anchors: a
     * short silence (2 to 6 ms). Admin only. Blocks while the hub writes
     * every unit.
     *
     * Errors: as [`Self::alink_system_save`], without `conflict`.
     */
    fun `alinkSystemAlign`(): AlinkSystemAlign
    
    /**
     * Recall scene `scene` (an id from the view) on every unit it names
     * (`system.scene.apply`). A recall the hub took but every device
     * refused (a sender holds one scene at a time) is answered with
     * `taken` false and the words to show, naming the frame it holds.
     * Control or admin.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `denied` when the role may not make one of
     * the scene's operations (the hub's words name it), `bad_request` for a
     * scene the model does not have; [`DspiError::Disconnected`].
     */
    fun `alinkSystemRecall`(`scene`: kotlin.String): AlinkSceneRecall
    
    /**
     * Save the "Clock and audio links" edits made on the view of `revision`, as
     * one `system.set` the hub takes only while its model is still at that
     * revision. Returns the new revision. Admin only.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Link`] `conflict` when the installation changed on
     * the hub meanwhile (nothing is saved; read the view again and edit
     * again), `denied` for another role, `bad_request` for values the hub
     * refuses, with its words; [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no session.
     */
    fun `alinkSystemSave`(`revision`: kotlin.ULong, `edits`: AlinkSystemEdits): kotlin.ULong
    
    /**
     * [`DspiCore::alink_system_save`] with the hub's whole answer: the new
     * revision and the warnings `system.set` gave about the model it kept.
     * Errors as there.
     */
    fun `alinkSystemSaveReport`(`revision`: kotlin.ULong, `edits`: AlinkSystemEdits): AlinkSystemSaved
    
    /**
     * The audio link's installation on the hub this session is on: its
     * elements with their alink state, the rooms, the scenes, alarms and a
     * one-line summary. Reads `system.status` (and `system.get` when the
     * model changed). The hub answers the status at most once a second per
     * session; asked sooner, this gives the last view again with `stale`.
     * Blocks on the network: keep it off a UI thread.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no hub session,
     * [`DspiError::Link`] with the hub's code or the client's.
     */
    fun `alinkSystemView`(): AlinkSystemView
    
    /**
     * Write the whole cached state to the device in one bulk transfer:
     * chunked `REQ_SET_ALL_PARAMS_CHUNK` (`0xA3`, at most 2048 bytes and
     * the transport's `max_transfer` per chunk), or single-shot
     * `REQ_SET_ALL_PARAMS` (`0xA1`) over a link without chunked bulk. The
     * device applies it on its main loop once the last byte lands.
     *
     * The write is bracketed with the connection's echo filter, so the
     * `BULK_INVALIDATED` it causes is recognised as our own. On success the
     * mirror is re-decoded from the image that was sent (the codec's clamps
     * applied).
     *
     * Runs under the transport's device lock (`Transport::lock`, a no-op
     * except on DSPi Link, where another console's changes are held off
     * for its duration; PLAN "Link device lock").
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device open, or, like the
     * old core, when no image has been read yet (nothing to apply: call
     * [`DspiCore::fetch_all`] first); [`DspiError::ReadOnly`] when the
     * firmware speaks another wire format; [`DspiError::Locked`] /
     * [`DspiError::Denied`] when the Link hub refuses its lock (nothing is
     * sent); the transport's typed error when a transfer fails (the device
     * then applies nothing).
     */
    fun `applyAllParams`()
    
    /**
     * `EXT_APPLY_AT_FRAME`: run `op` ([`APPLY_OP_MAX`] lists them) on the
     * samples of alink frame `frame`, heard `d_local` frames later on every
     * phase-locked unit at once; a frame already passed runs at once and
     * reports "late". Op 0 cancels every pending op (`frame` ignored). The
     * unit queues up to four.
     *
     * Errors as [`DspiCore::set_alink_slots`]; `InvalidArgument` for an
     * op above [`APPLY_OP_MAX`] or an `arg` or `value` outside its range
     * (unused ones 0; a preset slot 0-9, a master volume of -12800..0
     * hundredths of a dB, a rate code 0-2, an output below 9 and a delay of
     * 0-2047 samples).
     * `Refused` when the queue is full, the alink is not locked, or an
     * output or delay is past the device's.
     */
    fun `applyAtFrame`(`frame`: kotlin.ULong, `op`: kotlin.UByte, `arg`: kotlin.UByte, `value`: kotlin.Short)
    
    /**
     * `EXT_APPLY_AT_FRAME` on time base `time_base`: [`TIME_BASE_ALINK`]
     * (`frame` an alink frame, as [`DspiCore::apply_at_frame`]),
     * [`TIME_BASE_USB_SAMPLE`] (`frame` the unit's USB sample index S) or
     * [`TIME_BASE_BUS_FRAME`] (`frame` a bus frame 0-2047 in bits 0-10 and
     * the sample in it in bits 16-23: the same 8 bytes address the same
     * instant on every unit of one host, up to 1.98 s ahead). Ops and their
     * arguments are [`DspiCore::apply_at_frame`]'s.
     *
     * Errors, with no traffic: `Unsupported` for time base 0 without the
     * alink, 1 or 2 without the USB-direct block; `InvalidArgument` for
     * another time base, a bad bus-frame form, or an op or argument out of
     * range; `ReadOnly`, `Disconnected`. `Refused` when the unit stalls (not
     * phase-locked on the USB time base, a full queue).
     */
    fun `applyAtTimeBase`(`timeBase`: kotlin.UByte, `frame`: kotlin.ULong, `op`: kotlin.UByte, `arg`: kotlin.UByte, `value`: kotlin.Short)
    
    /**
     * Keep the change. Returns the state: [`GuardPhase::Kept`]; or, past
     * the timeout, the revert's end; or [`GuardPhase::Lost`] when the Keep
     * could not reach the hub (it is not sent again: the hub reverts).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when nothing is waiting.
     */
    fun `applyConfirm`(): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Begin a guard around a flow the client runs itself (output enables, a
     * control-surface edit, several hardware settings): capture a snapshot
     * now, then the client writes as usual and calls
     * [`DspiCore::apply_confirm`] or [`DspiCore::apply_revert`]; the timeout
     * and a lost connection work as for an import. `label` names the flow
     * in the status. Errors as [`DspiCore::apply_guarded_import`]'s before
     * anything is applied.
     */
    fun `applyGuardBegin`(`label`: kotlin.String, `timeoutS`: kotlin.UInt): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Whether an import or a recall is guarded by default here (owner,
     * OD-P1g): over DSPi Link to a hub that offers the guard, yes; on USB
     * it is the user's choice. An A/B toggle never is.
     */
    fun `applyGuardDefault`(): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Import `text` (a `.dspipreset` document) with a way back: capture a
     * snapshot, apply the blocks `options` and `eq_channels` choose (as
     * [`DspiCore::config_import_confirmed`] takes them), then wait
     * `timeout_s` seconds (0: 30, at most 590) for
     * [`DspiCore::apply_confirm`]. A timeout, [`DspiCore::apply_revert`] or a
     * failed import puts the snapshot back; over DSPi Link the hub guards it
     * too. No confirmation token: a guarded apply has its own way back.
     *
     * Returns the state, [`GuardPhase::Waiting`] with the changes.
     *
     * Errors, with nothing applied: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for a text
     * that is not a document, an empty selection, or while another guarded
     * apply waits; [`DspiError::Disconnected`], [`DspiError::ReadOnly`];
     * [`DspiError::Link`] for the hub's refusal (`busy`, `locked`,
     * `denied`, `no_device`). The import's own errors, after the snapshot
     * was put back (see [`DspiCore::apply_status`]).
     */
    fun `applyGuardedImport`(`text`: kotlin.String, `options`: kotlin.UInt, `eqChannels`: kotlin.UInt, `timeoutS`: kotlin.UInt): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Recall snapshot `id` from the store with a way back, as
     * [`DspiCore::apply_guarded_import`] does for a document. Errors as
     * there, and [`DspiCore::snapshot_recall`]'s.
     */
    fun `applyGuardedSnapshot`(`id`: kotlin.String, `timeoutS`: kotlin.UInt): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Recall a snapshot document's text with a way back (the web's, which
     * keeps snapshots as files the page holds). Only a snapshot: any other
     * document is refused with [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`], nothing sent.
     */
    fun `applyGuardedSnapshotDocument`(`text`: kotlin.String, `timeoutS`: kotlin.UInt): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Drive the guard: revert at the timeout, notice a lost connection, ask
     * the hub how a lost one ended once the connection is back. Call it
     * from the client's poll, off the UI thread (it may write). Returns the
     * state; at once, unchanged, while another guarded call runs.
     */
    fun `applyPoll`(): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Put the settings from before back now. Returns the state:
     * [`GuardPhase::Reverted`], [`GuardPhase::RevertFailed`], or
     * [`GuardPhase::Lost`] when the connection went. After a lost USB
     * connection, it recalls the captured snapshot once the same device is
     * open again (the user's word, never queued).
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] when nothing is waiting and no
     * lost apply can be reverted on the open device.
     */
    fun `applyRevert`(): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * The guard's state, for the countdown bar: no I/O, never blocks on a
     * guarded call.
     */
    fun `applyStatus`(): GuardedApply
    
    /**
     * Apply `profile` to wire channels `left` and `right` as
     * [`DspiCore::autoeq_preview`] shows it. First the device's state is
     * captured as a snapshot, returned as a document and filed in the
     * snapshot store when a folder is named ([`DspiCore::snapshot_set_dir`]);
     * then every write is one undo step labelled "AutoEQ: <name>": the
     * level when applied (`apply_level` as for the preview: inputs' preamp
     * by default, outputs' gain only when asked), any crossover band the
     * limits profile asks for, then every PEQ band of both channels.
     * Values past the limits profile are held to it and reported
     * ([`AutoEqPreview::held`]); nothing outside the pair changes.
     *
     * Errors: those of [`DspiCore::autoeq_preview`], then
     * [`DspiError::ReadOnly`], all before anything is sent; those of the
     * snapshot capture, before any write; then the errors of every setter
     * ([`DspiError::LimitExceeded`] for a high-pass the limits profile
     * cannot place, [`DspiError::Transfer`] and the rest). A failure part
     * way leaves the writes made so far as the one undo step, and the
     * snapshot filed (with a folder named).
     */
    fun `autoeqApply`(`profile`: AutoEqProfile, `left`: kotlin.UByte, `right`: kotlin.UByte, `applyLevel`: kotlin.Boolean?): AutoEqApply
    
    /**
     * What applying `profile` to wire channels `left` and `right` (two
     * inputs or two outputs) would do: each channel's PEQ bank, level,
     * crossover and response curve, what the limits profile would hold, and
     * the lines to show. Reads the mirror only: nothing is sent.
     *
     * `apply_level`: whether the profile's preamp is written; `None` is the
     * default: yes on inputs (their preamp), no on outputs (their
     * calibrated gain is kept and the preamp only suggested,
     * [`AutoEqPreview::suggested_trim_db`]). `Some(true)` on outputs writes
     * it as their gain.
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::Disconnected`]; [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`]
     * for a channel out of range, the same channel twice, an input with an
     * output, or a profile value no setter would take (a non-finite preamp,
     * a known filter with a frequency or Q not above 0).
     */
    fun `autoeqPreview`(`profile`: AutoEqProfile, `left`: kotlin.UByte, `right`: kotlin.UByte, `applyLevel`: kotlin.Boolean?): AutoEqPreview
    
    /**
     * Begin an undo group: everything written until the matching
     * [`DspiCore::end_group`] that undo can reverse is one undo step,
     * carrying `label` ([`HistoryEntry::label`]), as a drag is. Use it for
     * one action that writes several values (a linked pair, a Clear All, a
     * routing preset). Groups nest: only the outermost begin starts the
     * step and takes its label, and only the matching end ends it. The
     * group belongs to the connection: a disconnect, another device
     * opened, a re-attach, a re-read ([`DspiCore::fetch_all`]) and a change
     * that ends the history (a preset load, a bulk image, a factory reset)
     * end it at any depth, and an `end_group` after that does nothing.
     * With no device the call is ignored. An empty group records nothing.
     * A write undo cannot reverse stands apart, as in a drag. The group is
     * the connection's, not the thread's: call it on the thread that sends
     * the group's writes, in order with them.
     *
     * A group begun while a drag is marked ([`DspiCore::set_dragging`])
     * joins the drag's step instead of ending it (the step takes the
     * group's label if it had none), and its `end_group` leaves that step
     * open until the drag ends: one drag is one step.
     */
    fun `beginGroup`(`label`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * The device's name as last known, with no traffic, kept as
     * [`DspiCore::cached_input_source_name`] keeps source names. "" when
     * unnamed. Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without device names;
     * `Disconnected`.
     */
    fun `cachedDeviceName`(): kotlin.String
    
    /**
     * The name of input source `source` as last known, with no traffic: read
     * on connect, kept current by notifications (from any client) and by
     * [`DspiCore::set_input_source_name`], and read again after a lost
     * notification. "" when unnamed. For a client's rows, which would
     * otherwise read over the wire what connect has just read.
     *
     * Errors: `Unsupported` on firmware without source names; `InvalidArgument`
     * for a source past the table; `Disconnected` without a device or before
     * it was probed.
     */
    fun `cachedInputSourceName`(`source`: kotlin.UByte): kotlin.String
    
    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::redo`] has a change to apply again. False with no
     * device.
     */
    fun `canRedo`(): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Whether [`DspiCore::undo`] has a change to reverse. False with no
     * device.
     */
    fun `canUndo`(): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Take the live state as what the active preset holds: from now on
     * [`DspiCore::preset_changes`] lists what differs from it. The core
     * does this itself after a connect, a load, a save, a revert and a
     * factory reset. Without a device, or without an image in this build's
     * format, there is no baseline.
     */
    fun `captureBaseline`()
    
    /**
     * Whether enabling `output` would conflict with Core 1's mode (PDM on
     * the last output and the Core 1 EQ worker exclude each other, and the
     * firmware skips a blocked enable silently). One `REQ_GET_CORE1_CONFLICT`
     * read; a failed read is an error, never "no conflict".
     *
     * Errors: [`DspiError::InvalidArgument`] for an output past the
     * device's output count (`DspState.num_output_channels`);
     * [`DspiError::Disconnected`] with no device; a stall, timeout or other
     * transfer error from the read.
     */
    fun `checkCore1Conflict`(`output`: kotlin.UByte): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Clear `EXT_ALINK_USB_DIAG`. Errors as [`DspiCore::set_alink_usb`].
     */
    fun `clearAlinkUsbDiag`()
    
    /**
     * Clear the device's sticky clip flags (`REQ_CLEAR_CLIPS`, which reads
     * the flags back and clears them; the value is not used).
     *
     * Meter housekeeping, not a parameter: it touches no setting and
     * nothing in the bulk image, so it is allowed on a read-only
     * connection too. This is the one named exception (PLAN §1.2) to
     * "state-changing opcodes only on a `Writable`": it goes out on a plain
     * `Connection`.
